<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Special settlements in the Soviet Union</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as special settlers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Special settlements were places of forced residence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> to which whole classes and nationalities were deported, a method of political repression separate from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> labour camps. From 1930 peasants branded kulaks were sent with their families to remote parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, Kazakhstan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>; from 1940 people from annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the Baltic States followed, and in 1941–1944 whole peoples, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga-germans">Volga Germans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a>, Ingush, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmyks">Kalmyks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a>. Settlers lived in families under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> watch, barred from many jobs and from going home. On 1 January 1953 there were 2,753,356 deported and special settlers; the system was abolished in the 1950s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/special-settlements-in-the-soviet-union.jpg" alt="Special settlements in the Soviet Union" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uskut.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Political and administrative exile had existed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a>, and the first Soviet decrees on labour camps already spoke of using settlers to colonise remote lands. In 1929 Stalin's government named districts of Western Siberia for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of socially dangerous classes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. After the grain crisis of 1928 and forced collectivisation, peasants were deported in large numbers, and a joint decree of 1 February 1930 ordered wide-scale dekulakization. On Nazino Island near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomsk">Tomsk</a> in 1933 more than 4,000 deportees died or disappeared within thirteen weeks, given only raw flour to live on.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1939 the lands annexed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact">Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</a> were cleared of supposed social enemies; estimates of deported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> range from 400,000 to 1.9 million, including prisoners of war. On 23 June 1940 Lavrenty Beria ordered Murmansk Oblast cleansed of foreign nationals, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> from the Baltic States, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> and Northern Bukovina followed in May and June 1941. During the war whole peoples of enemy nationality were deported as a precaution, and in 1943–1944 peoples declared guilty of collaboration, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkars">Balkars</a>, Karachays, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meskhetian-turks">Meskhetian Turks</a> and Crimean Bolgars.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">Deportations</a> went on after 1945: 35,000 people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> and Northern Bukovina in July 1949, Iranians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in 1950, and about 9,400 Jehovah's Witnesses, about 4,000 of them children, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> in Operation North in 1951. Kolkhozniks convicted as parasites under a decree of 2 June 1942, the ukazniks, received eight years of exile.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Archival data for 1 January 1950 counted 2,572,829 settlers, according to an MVD document quoted by Dmitri Volkogonov. The historian J. Otto Pohl has described the special settlements as a form of apartheid. When the system was abolished, most deported peoples could return home, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meskhetian-turks">Meskhetian Turks</a> were denied the right of return under Khrushchev and Brezhnev and largely remained where they had been sent, held there by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/propiska-in-the-soviet-union">propiska</a> system. The special settlement restriction on members of persecuted religious groups was lifted only in September 1965.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Special settlements in the Soviet Union” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The deported and their families, “<b>special settlers</b>” as the government called them, lived on starvation rations, and many tried to escape.</blockquote><a href="/societies/soviet-union">Social Hierarchy of Stalin's Soviet Union</a></li></ul></section>
